  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/279c3cd6084423d913186f4ba467ea50d0f794cf54773785777cb8841f5c2ca1.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-3,-1]$\n- $x\\in [-1,3]$\n- $x\\in [0,1]$",
  "worked_solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The given function is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-3,-1]$ and on $x\\in [0,1].$  Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/0388335095ed530ce07d278f82be5eb3bff14859db64b6e9ec47da27861bb657.png)\n\n    \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval. $x\\in [-1,3]$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938271/assets/522b5661aaf9b7640c305b8cefd06f49cc8eed590f3ae736fc6720e6165bc3a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
